    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">The Film Connection</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">In 2008, James Cameron's production team visited Zhangjiajie while scouting visual references for Pandora's floating mountain landscapes. The sandstone pillars — particularly the "Southern Sky Column" in Yuanjiajie — became the direct inspiration for the Hallelujah Mountains in the 2009 film. After Avatar's release, the Chinese park administration officially renamed the Southern Sky Column to "Avatar Hallelujah Mountain" in January 2010. <a href="https://www.bbc.com/travel/article/20200113-how-avatar-changed-a-chinese-mountain" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">BBC Travel covered this story in detail</a>.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">The renaming was controversial among locals who preferred the original name, but it was undeniably effective: annual visitor numbers to Zhangjiajie tripled in the five years following the film's release. Today, you will find Avatar-themed signage, merchandise stalls, and a life-sized banshee model at the Yuanjiajie viewing area. It is touristy, but the pillar itself is genuinely spectacular regardless of the branding. On our first visit in 2022, we stood there feeling slightly cynical about the whole Avatar gimmick — and then the mist rolled in and the pillar started to float, and we understood exactly why Cameron picked this place.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/feature-1.webp" type="image/webp" />
          <img
            src="/feature-1.jpg"
            alt="Sandstone pillars of Zhangjiajie rising through morning mist, similar to the Avatar Hallelujah Mountain formation"
            class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover"
            width="800"
            height="600"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </picture>
      </div>
