    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Where to Find It</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Avatar Hallelujah Mountain is inside Yuanjiajie Scenic Area, which is part of the Zhangjiajie National Forest Park core zone. From the park's Wulingyuan entrance, take the free internal shuttle bus to the Yuanjiajie stop — about 15 minutes. From there, it is a well-paved 15-minute walk to the main viewing platform. The pillar stands alone in a clearing, unmistakable at 1,080 meters, with a dedicated platform for photos.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">The easiest way to reach Yuanjiajie is via the <a href="/guide#park-zones" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">Bailong Elevator</a> — the world's tallest outdoor lift at 326 meters. Take it up from the Wulingyuan side, and the Yuanjiajie trailhead is a 5-minute walk from the exit. The elevator costs CNY 72 one-way and the ride takes under 2 minutes, but the view as you emerge from the cliff face into open sky is worth every yuan. Our full <a href="/guide" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">guide page</a> has the complete park layout with shuttle routes and elevator details.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">When to Go</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">Early morning (before 9 AM) or late afternoon (after 4 PM). The main platform gets dense crowds from 9:30 AM to 3:30 PM during peak season — we have seen queues of 30+ people waiting to take a photo at the railing. In April 2026, we arrived at 7:30 AM and had the platform to ourselves for about 20 minutes before the first tour groups arrived.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">Misty mornings create the best atmosphere: when clouds fill the valley below, the pillar appears to float. Spring (March-May) and autumn (September-November) offer the best mist conditions — check our <a href="/best-time-to-visit" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">best time to visit guide</a> for month-by-month weather breakdowns.</p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-5 text-sm text-stone-600">
        <strong class="text-stone-800">Personal note:</strong> On our November 2025 visit, we arrived at 7 AM in heavy fog and could barely see 20 meters ahead. By 7:30, the sun burned through and the mist settled at the pillar's base — it was the perfect floating effect. That 30-minute window produced our favorite photos from three trips.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">The Secret Viewpoint</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">Most visitors crowd the main railing, snap a photo, and leave. Walk 200 meters further along the trail (toward the First Bridge Under Heaven direction) and you will find a smaller, unmarked viewpoint with essentially the same angle and typically zero people.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">We found this spot on our second visit by accident — we were trying to escape the crowd at main platform and just kept walking. On a busy Saturday in April 2026, we stood there for 20 minutes without a single other person stopping. The trail is well-paved and easy to find: just follow the main path past the Avatar pillar toward the First Bridge Under Heaven sign. The opening appears on your right about two minutes past the last tour-group selfie spot.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Photography Tips</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">For more detailed gear recommendations, see our <a href="/photography" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">full photography guide</a>. Here are the specific settings that worked for us on the Avatar pillar:</p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-stone-500 text-sm">
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-forest-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5">&bull;</span> <strong class="text-stone-700">Mist is your friend.</strong> A clear-sky photo of the pillar is just a tall rock. Mist curling around the base creates the floating effect. Check the weather forecast for morning humidity and go on days with >70% humidity for the best chance.</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-forest-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5">&bull;</span> <strong class="text-stone-700">Shoot vertical.</strong> The pillar is nearly 1.1 km tall. Landscape orientation crops the top and bottom. Portrait orientation shows the full height — we shoot all our pillar photos in portrait now after learning this the hard way.</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-forest-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5">&bull;</span> <strong class="text-stone-700">Wait for the gap.</strong> Tour groups move in pulses — a new group arrives every 5-10 minutes, takes photos for 3 minutes, then moves on. If you wait 5 minutes, you get a 2-minute window with an empty railing. This works at both the main and secret viewpoints.</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-forest-600 shrink-0 mt-0.5">&bull;</span> <strong class="text-stone-700">Use a polarizing filter.</strong> The pillar is quartz-sandstone and highly reflective in direct sunlight. A polarizer cuts the glare and brings out the natural color of the rock — it makes a dramatic difference, especially with the subtropical greenery behind it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
